{"name":"Poetic Light","hex":"#e2ded8","fetched":"2026-10-12","looked":{"definitions":[{"term":"poetic","whole":false,"parts":[{"part":"Adjective","definitions":["Relating to poetry.","Characteristic of poets; romantic, imaginative, etc."]}],"link":"https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/poetic"},{"term":"light","whole":false,"parts":[{"part":"Noun","definitions":["Electromagnetic radiation in the wavelength range visible to the human eye (about 400–750 nanometers): visible light.","Electromagnetic radiation in the wavelength range visible to the human eye or in nearby ranges (infrared or ultraviolet radiation)."]},{"part":"Verb","definitions":["To start (a fire).","To set fire to; to set burning."]}],"link":"https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/light"}],"entries":[{"title":"Poetry","blocks":[{"text":"Poetry is a form of literary art that uses aesthetic and often rhythmic qualities of language to evoke meanings in addition to, or in place of, literal or surface-level meanings. A particular instance of poetry is a poem and is written by a poet. Poets use a variety of techniques called poetic devices, such as assonance, alliteration, consonance, euphony and cacophony, onomatopoeia, rhythm, rhyme schemes and sound symbolism, to produce musical or other artistic effects. They also frequently organize these devices into poetic structures, which may be strict or loose, conventional or invented by the poet. Poetic structures vary dramatically by language and cultural convention, but they often rely on rhythmic metre: patterns of syllable stress or syllable weight. They may also use repeating patterns of phonemes, phoneme groups, tones, words, or entire phrases. Poetic structures may even be semantic."}],"link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Poetry","wikidata":"Q482"},{"title":"Light","blocks":[{"text":"Light, visible light, or visible radiation is electromagnetic radiation that can be perceived by the human eye. Visible light spans the visible spectrum and is usually defined as having wavelengths in the range of 400–700 nanometers (nm), corresponding to frequencies of 750–420 terahertz. The visible band sits adjacent to the infrared and the ultraviolet, called collectively optical radiation."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/f/fa/Prism_flat_rainbow_%28cropped%29.jpg/330px-Prism_flat_rainbow_%28cropped%29.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Light","wikidata":"Q9128"}],"images":[{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/f/f6/Books_and_Scroll_Ornament_with_Open_Book.png/500px-Books_and_Scroll_Ornament_with_Open_Book.png?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/f/f6/Books_and_Scroll_Ornament_with_Open_Book.png/500px-Books_and_Scroll_Ornament_with_Open_Book.png?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail","alt":"","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File%3ABooks_and_Scroll_Ornament_with_Open_Book.png"},{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/c/cd/The_oldest_love_poem._Sumerian_terracotta_tablet_from_Nippur%2C_Iraq._Ur_III_period%2C_2037-2029_BCE._Ancient_Orient_Museum%2C_Istanbul.jpg/250px-The_oldest_love_poem._Sumerian_terracotta_tablet_from_Nippur%2C_Iraq._Ur_III_period%2C_2037-2029_BCE._Ancient_Orient_Museum%2C_Istanbul.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x, https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/c/cd/The_oldest_love_poem._Sumerian_terracotta_tablet_from_Nippur%2C_Iraq._Ur_III_period%2C_2037-2029_BCE._Ancient_Orient_Museum%2C_Istanbul.jpg/330px-The_oldest_love_poem._Sumerian_terracotta_tablet_from_Nippur%2C_Iraq._Ur_III_period%2C_2037-2029_BCE._Ancient_Orient_Museum%2C_Istanbul.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 2x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/c/cd/The_oldest_love_po